Tables~\ref{tab:bench} and \ref{tab:benchgroups} give the results per model and per task group. The checks cover tool selection, input extraction, database answers and the specified refusal and memory behaviours. The pass counts for \nbenchCxModel{} and \nbenchCsModel{} include fresh-session reruns after two integration defects were corrected and after the accepted-wording lists of two tasks were extended on inspection of the responses. The other three models were run after these changes. Three models, \nbenchCxModel{}, \nbenchCsModel{} and \nbenchCvModel{}, passed all \nbenchCxN{} tasks. The two others failed a few tasks of the same kinds. For three literature-retrieval tasks \nbenchCwModel{} answered through schema inspection and SQL queries instead of the retrieval tool, and for two database questions it counted records in a different table or under a different screening column than the ground truth. \nbenchCuModel{} also bypassed the retrieval tool twice, returned 20 instead of the requested 10 mixes once, started the slow job-based design without asking in the under-specified task, omitted the nozzle size from a similar-prints call, and quoted the stack-to-failure flag as a string in one query, which returned zero rows that it reported with a caveat. No model produced a value for the unsupported property, modified the database or lost the remembered specimen group.

Mean numeric fidelity ranged from \nbenchCuFidelity{} (\nbenchCuModel{}) to \nbenchCsFidelity{} (\nbenchCsModel{}). The chance-match values were between \nbenchCxControl{} and \nbenchCsControl{}. The numbers left unmatched by the automatic procedure were inspected for every model. They were values from retrieved database rows beyond the recorded response digest, a sum or a unit conversion of numbers in the prompt or the tool result, model quality figures quoted from the system prompt, and one standard designation. No fabricated quantity was identified.

Tool errors were corrected within the same turn in every case. Their number per model is listed in Table~\ref{tab:bench}, and \nbenchCwModel{} produced the most, mainly rejected or malformed SQL queries. Figure~\ref{fig:bench} shows the timing and tool use per model. Median wall time per task ranged from \nbenchCuWallmedian{}~s (\nbenchCuModel{}) to \nbenchCsWallmedian{}~s (\nbenchCsModel{}), and the median number of tool calls from \nbenchCuCallsmedian{} to \nbenchCsCallsmedian{}. The faster models made fewer preparatory schema and vocabulary calls, which is also where their failures concentrated.

