196: \subsection{Agent core}
197: 
198: The system prompt is assembled for each turn from the current model information and session context. It contains supported targets, training sizes, validation quality, weak-model flags, material vocabulary, specimen groups, curing presets and input examples. Saved user preferences and a session summary are also included.
199: 
200: The agent rules require quantitative predictions to come from tool results and to include units. Predictions are reported with $q_{50}$ and lower and upper interval bounds $q_{10}$ and $q_{90}$. Weak predictions and training-range violations must be identified, and assumed defaults must be stated. Database questions require schema inspection and a distinction between paper and mix counts. Expensive searches require specified targets and a search space. Otherwise, the agent asks for clarification before proceeding.
201: 
202: For an unsuccessful buildability assessment, the agent must report available schedule or material alternatives, including required $\tau_{s,0}$ or $A_{\mathrm{thix}}$. Tool errors are explained, corrected and retried at most twice. The prompt states that the property models describe correlations in published data and require experimental verification.
203: 
204: The tool loop ends when the model stops requesting tools or reaches twelve iterations. Session transcripts and a rolling summary preserve conversational context. Older content is summarised when the transcript exceeds 40\,000 characters. User preferences saved through the memory tool enter later sessions.
205: 
206: \subsection{LLM models and access}
207: 
208: Five OpenAI models were evaluated through the Codex command-line interface (version 0.163) with the tools served over MCP: \nbenchCxModel{} (the interface default), \nbenchCsModel{}, \nbenchCuModel{}, \nbenchCvModel{} and \nbenchCwModel{}, the last being a previous-generation model. Each model used its default reasoning setting, and no prompt was tuned to a model. The set spans one model family only, so it tests whether the agent design holds across models of different size and generation within that family, not across families. Web and terminal interfaces share the agent core, and external clients can access the registry through MCP.
