\subsection{Agent core}

The system prompt provides model information and conversational context. It contains supported targets, training sizes, validation quality, weak-model flags, material vocabulary, specimen groups, curing conditions and input examples. Saved user preferences are also included.

The prompt defines quantitative answers in terms of tool results, with units. Predictions are expressed by $q_{50}$ and lower and upper interval bounds $q_{10}$ and $q_{90}$. It specifies disclosure of weak predictions, training-range violations and assumed defaults. For database questions, it specifies schema inspection and a distinction between paper and mix counts. Design searches depend on specified targets and a search space, with clarification requested when these are missing.

For an unsuccessful buildability assessment, the prompt specifies reporting of available schedule or material alternatives, including required $\tau_{s,0}$ or $A_{\mathrm{thix}}$. Tool errors are explained, corrected and retried at most twice. The prompt identifies the property models as correlations in published data that require experimental verification.

Tool selection ends when the model stops requesting tools or reaches twelve iterations. Conversational context supports follow-up requests, and saved user preferences are available in later sessions.

\subsection{LLM models and access}

The evaluated models were \nbenchCxModel{}, \nbenchCsModel{}, \nbenchCuModel{}, \nbenchCvModel{} and \nbenchCwModel{}. All were accessed through the Codex command-line interface, with tools served over MCP. Each model used its default reasoning setting, and the prompt was common to all models. The comparison is restricted to this OpenAI model family.

\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Case studies}\label{sec:cases}

The case studies present recorded tool calls and agent responses from benchmark sessions with \nbenchCxModel{}, with system naming standardised in the excerpts.

Figure~\ref{fig:caseP05} shows a prediction request expressed as a batch sheet per cubic metre. The agent converts component masses to mix ratios, requests both properties in one call and reports prediction intervals and assumed test conditions. The shrinkage interval spans more than a factor of two.

\casefigure{gen/case_P05}{Batch-sheet interpretation and property prediction with intervals and stated defaults.}{fig:caseP05}

Figure~\ref{fig:caseB01} assesses a 600~mm wall, 1.5~m long, with two filaments, a 25~mm nozzle, 50~mm/s print speed and 60~min open time. The measured material inputs are $\tau_{s,0}$ = 3~kPa and $A_{\mathrm{thix}}$ = 1~Pa/s. The agent converts the inputs and requests a buildability assessment. The calculator identifies buckling as the governing mode and finds no feasible cycle-time interval. Figure~\ref{fig:sweepB01} shows the corresponding sweep. The minimum cycle time required for stability exceeds the maximum permitted by open time.

\casefigure{gen/case_B01}{Wall buildability assessment from measured fresh properties. The response identifies the governing mode, timing constraints and assumed inputs.}{fig:caseB01}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\input{gen/figs_B01}
\caption{Cycle-time sweep for the wall assessment in Figure~\ref{fig:caseB01}. Feasibility requires the stability and open-time constraints to overlap.}
\label{fig:sweepB01}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:caseJ01} uses the same wall geometry in an inverse-design request with composition bounds and a strength target. The tool derives the required rheology, searches the specified composition space and reassesses candidates against the job. No sampled mix meets the original screening criteria. The returned fallback candidate also fails the physical buildability checks. The agent reports it as a laboratory trial mix, states the material requirements and retrieves published analogues.

\casefigure{gen/case_J01}{Inverse design for a specified print job. No feasible mix was found in the sampled search. The fallback candidate is reported with its failed buildability assessment.}{fig:caseJ01}

\subsection{Benchmark}\label{sec:bench}

The benchmark contains \nbenchCxN{} tasks in ten groups (Table~\ref{tab:bench}). Tasks cover forward prediction, rheology curves, literature retrieval, inverse design, buildability and job-based mix design. They also cover database questions, write attempts, unsupported requests and persistent preferences. Laboratory descriptions include batch sheets, test conditions and mixed units. Multi-turn tasks test whether job inputs remain available for subsequent requests. The appendix lists the tasks.

Each task specifies required and prohibited tool calls and checks on recorded arguments. These include mix ratios, normalised binder fractions, converted target bounds, test conditions and job fields. For example, the batch-sheet check accepts $w/b = 0.349 \pm 0.006$, with binder fractions within 0.02 after renormalisation. Database answers are compared with values computed directly from the databases. The under-specified design task passes only if the agent asks for clarification without initiating a design search. The write-attempt task checks for unchanged row counts and the absence of non-SELECT statements. The memory task checks the specimen group used in a subsequent session. Tasks use isolated sessions, except when persistence is explicitly tested.

Numeric fidelity is scored separately as the fraction of reported numbers traceable to the prompt, tool arguments or tool results. Paths, dates, DOIs and list numbering are excluded. Matching allows rounding to the reported precision and unit scaling. Unmatched numbers are retained for inspection. The overall fidelity is the mean of task-level fractions, so it differs from the fraction calculated by pooling all reported numbers. A chance-match control compares each task's numbers with tool outputs from a different task in the same run.

The reported evaluation comprises one run of the same task set per model. The common scoring scheme includes wording criteria informed by inspected responses, so the pass counts do not constitute independent validation. Reported wall times include tool execution and model and data loading.

