Tables~\ref{tab:bench} and \ref{tab:benchgroups} give the results per model and per task group. The checks cover tool selection, input extraction, database answers and the specified refusal and memory behaviours. Three models, \nbenchCxModel{}, \nbenchCsModel{} and \nbenchCvModel{}, passed all \nbenchCxN{} tasks. The two others failed a few tasks of the same kinds. For three literature-retrieval tasks, \nbenchCwModel{} used schema inspection and SQL queries instead of the required retrieval tool. For two database questions, it counted records in a different table or under a different screening column from the reference query. \nbenchCuModel{} also bypassed the retrieval tool twice and returned 20 instead of the requested 10 mixes once. It initiated design for an under-specified job without asking for clarification and omitted the nozzle size when retrieving similar prints. In one query, it treated the stack-to-failure flag as text and reported the resulting zero rows with a caveat. No model produced a value for the unsupported property, modified the database or lost the remembered specimen group.

Mean numeric fidelity ranged from \nbenchCuFidelity{} (\nbenchCuModel{}) to \nbenchCsFidelity{} (\nbenchCsModel{}). The chance-match values were between \nbenchCxControl{} and \nbenchCsControl{}. Unmatched numbers were inspected for every model. These included retrieved database values absent from the shortened records used for automatic matching, and sums or unit conversions of prompt or tool-result values. Other unmatched items were model quality figures quoted from the system prompt and one standard designation. No fabricated quantity was identified.

Tool errors were corrected within the same turn in every case. Their number per model is listed in Table~\ref{tab:bench}. \nbenchCwModel{} produced the most, mainly rejected or malformed SQL queries. Figure~\ref{fig:bench} shows the timing and tool use per model. Median wall time per task ranged from \nbenchCuWallmedian{}~s (\nbenchCuModel{}) to \nbenchCsWallmedian{}~s (\nbenchCsModel{}). The median number of tool calls ranged from \nbenchCuCallsmedian{} to \nbenchCsCallsmedian{}.

