\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

The case studies show how natural-language requests connect material predictions with process constraints. In the wall example, the agent reports an infeasible schedule and an unsuccessful design search. This result illustrates the need to retain uncertainty and failed checks when presenting candidate mixes. Related structural-analysis and monitoring systems also connect language models to domain tools \cite{liu2026llm4shm,avila2025hai}. Here, the evaluation concerns orchestration of literature-trained property models and buildability calculations.

Numeric fidelity measures whether reported numbers can be traced to the available inputs and outputs. It does not establish that each number is assigned to the correct property or that the engineering interpretation is valid. The chance-match control provides a comparison for numerical overlap. Automatic matching is limited by incomplete records of tool responses, so some values require manual verification.

The rheology intervals limit inverse design. Nominal 80\,\% intervals for static yield stress, $A_{\mathrm{thix}}$ and flow can exceed a decade. Published rheological measurements combine different devices and rest protocols. Protocol-normalised targets are needed to assess whether this variability can be reduced. The present search outcomes support candidate screening and schedule assessment, with experimental verification required before printing.

The buildability calculator uses linear structuration and a free-wall buckling criterion. Prints longer than about 90~min and braced or closed paths require further assessment of structuration laws and section-specific stability. In particular, treating closed sections as not buckling-critical is a modelling assumption. The empirical collapse indicator is derived from the observed collapse-ratio distribution. Its calibration as a failure probability for new jobs has not been established by the reported benchmark.

The benchmark evaluates specified tool calls and answer checks. It does not measure engineering adequacy through independent expert assessment or printing experiments. The five models belong to one family, and each was evaluated once. Performance with other families, repeated runs and unfamiliar user phrasing remains to be evaluated. Failures in literature retrieval and database questions accounted for most failed tasks in Table~\ref{tab:benchgroups}. These results do not isolate the causes of the model differences. Tool descriptions were written in Korean and the benchmark prompts were in English, which may also affect results.

The registry can expose additional computational functions, such as durability or life-cycle assessment models. Transfer to these tasks would require suitable tools, task definitions and validation. Practitioner studies are needed to determine whether the conversational interface improves planning accuracy or effort.

\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusion}

3DCP-Agent connects natural-language requests to \nnModels{} property models, inverse mix design, buildability calculations and literature retrieval through a registry of \nnTools{} tools. The case studies demonstrate batch-sheet interpretation and assessment of a specified wall, including an unsuccessful mix search. Three of five OpenAI models passed all \nbenchCxN{} benchmark tasks. The two others failed between five and six tasks, mostly by answering retrieval tasks through SQL or by misreading the database schema. Mean numeric fidelity was between \nbenchCuFidelity{} and \nbenchCsFidelity{} across models, and no fabricated quantity was found on inspection. These results concern the tested agent interactions. Wide rheology intervals and the buildability assumptions limit the resulting design recommendations. Further work requires protocol-normalised material data, independent task evaluation and experimental printing validation.

\section*{Declaration of generative AI and AI-assisted technologies in the manuscript preparation process}
During manuscript preparation, the author used Claude (Anthropic) for software development, benchmark execution, drafting and editing. Claude also assisted in drawing the explanatory schematics in Figures~\ref{fig:arch}, \ref{fig:seq}, \ref{fig:inverse} and \ref{fig:criteria} from the author's specifications. GPT-6-Astra (OpenAI) assisted with editorial review and layout drafts of those schematics. The author reviewed and edited the content as needed and takes full responsibility for the content of the published article.

\section*{Data availability}
The software, trained models, both databases, benchmark tasks, evaluation code and recorded transcripts are available at \url{https://github.com/solmoipark/3DCP_ERC}.

\section*{Declaration of competing interest}
The author declares no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.

